statement:
There are `n` balloons in a row and `n` bullets. Balloon `i` has a score `a[i]`. You shoot the balloons one at a time, in any order you choose, until all of them are gone. After a balloon is shot it disappears, and its former neighbours become adjacent.

The score starts at zero. When you shoot a balloon, the score increases by an amount that depends on the balloons **currently remaining** next to it:

- if it has both a left neighbour and a right neighbour, add the **product** of the two neighbours' scores;
- if it has only a left neighbour (it is the rightmost remaining balloon), add the left neighbour's score;
- if it has only a right neighbour (it is the leftmost remaining balloon), add the right neighbour's score;
- if it is the only balloon left, add its **own** score.

The shot balloon's own score is not used in the first three cases. Find the **maximum total score** over all shooting orders.

This is a variant of the classic Burst Balloons problem in which the missing neighbours at the ends are handled by the rules above instead of by imaginary balloons.

